TI  - Clinical evaluation of seven cases of PCP in cancer patients
AU  - Takahashi T.
AU  - Nishioka M.
AU  - Goda Y.
AU  - Kojima A.
AU  - Shima K.
AU  - Yagi Y.
AU  - Fuseya Y.
AU  - Shoji T.
AU  - Katakura H.
AU  - Sakai N.
T2  - Respirology
T3  - 22nd Congress of the Asian Pacific Society of Respirology, ASPR 2017. Sydney, NSW Australia.
AB  - Background and Aim: Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP) is a life-threatening infection and seen increasingly in non-HIV infected immunocompromised populations, especially in cancer patients. However, very few big surveys performed about non-HIV PCP in cancer patients and its clinical features are not established. Method(s): We retrospectively reviewed the clinical features of non-HIV PCP in our department from April 2013 to March 2017. Seven patients were enrolled. Result(s): All Men, the mean age was 72 years old. Three patients had lung cancer, 2 had colon cancer, 1 had pancreatic cancer, and the other had parotid grand cancer. Four patients were treated with chemotherapy, and other three patients were prescribed oral corticosteroids. Bronchos-copy was performed in 2 cases, but not in other cases because of unstable respiratory symptoms. beta-D glucan serum levels were assessed for diagnose in all cases. All cases were treated with corticosteroids and 4 cases received high dose corticosteroid therapy. 2 patients died for PCP. Conclusion(s): PCP infection is not rare in cancer patients. It is difficult to distinguish PCP from drug induced interstitial pneumonia, because some anticancer agents induce interstitial pneumonia. If it is diagnosed properly, patients can survive.
