TI  - Low-dose atovaquone for prophylaxis of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia in patients with connective tissue diseases: an open-label, multicentre, single-arm, phase II trial.
AU  - Ida, Tomoaki
AU  - Furuta, Shunsuke
AU  - Inaba, Yosuke
AU  - Iwamoto, Taro
AU  - Kameda, Shuhei
AU  - Kobayashi, Yoshihisa
AU  - Takahashi, Kohei
AU  - Takahashi, Tatsuro
AU  - Tamachi, Tomohiro
AU  - Yasui, Masahiro
AU  - Nakajima, Hiroshi
T2  - EULAR Rheumatology Open
AB  - Objectives: Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP) is a life-threatening complication that occurs in immunocompromised individuals, including patients with connective tissue diseases under glucocorticoid treatments. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX) and atovaquone 1500 mg/d, the first and one of the second-line prophylactic options of PCP, are often difficult to continue the administration due to side effects or the cost. This trial aimed to investigate the efficacy and safety of low-dose atovaquone (750 mg/d) for prophylaxis of PCP in patients with connective tissue disease who started glucocorticoid treatments., Methods: This open-label, multicentre, single-arm, phase II trial included patients with connective tissue diseases who were scheduled to start or within 10 days from the initiation of 0.5 mg/kg/d or more of prednisolone or equivalent dose. All patients received low-dose atovaquone for 24 weeks. The primary endpoint was the incidence of PCP at 24 weeks, and adverse events were also assessed as secondary endpoints., Results: Fifty patients were assigned to receive low-dose atovaquone, and 45 were included in the primary analysis. Thirteen patients (28.9%) had comorbidities of lung diseases, and 33 (73.3%) received one or more immunosuppressants or biologics in addition to glucocorticoids during the trial period. No cases of PCP occurred up to 24 weeks. There were 7 nonserious and 2 serious adverse events, including 2 nonserious adverse events possibly related to low-dose atovaquone., Conclusions: Results of this trial suggest that low-dose atovaquone has sufficient efficacy for the prophylaxis of PCP and a good safety profile in patients with connective tissue diseases under glucocorticoid treatments. Copyright © 2025 The Author(s).
