T2  - Mycoses
T3  - 8th Congress on Trends in Medical Mycology, TIMM 2017. Belgrade Serbia.
AB  - Objective: Description of case of successful treatment of pneumocystis pneumonia (PP) and invasive pulmonary aspergillosis (IA) in patient with Hodgkin's lymphoma. Material(s) and Method(s): For the IA and PCP diagnosis criteria EORTS/ MSG, 2008 were used. Pneumocystis pneumonia was confirmed by positive "Monfluo" test in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BAL). Result(s): Patient M., 27 years old, was admitted to the hematology department of the Leningrad Regional Clinical Hospital in September 2016 for cytostatic chemotherapy. The patient was suffering from Hodgkin's lymphoma, stage 2 B X with mediastinum damage, intrathoracic, and supraclavicular lymph nodes involvement, since 2015 y. In In May 2016, he received two courses of cytostatic chemotherapy (ABVD). Remission lymphoma has not been achieved. During this hospitalization cytostatic chemotherapy (BEACOPP Esc.) was started. Antifungal prophylaxis was not performed. The patient had postcytostatic pancytopenia (lymphopenia- 0.37x109/l), infectious syndrome with lung injury symptoms. On CT scan numerous interstitial lesions, and peribronchial foci of infiltration in the parenchyma of the lower lobes of both lungs were revealed. Fibrobronchoscopy was performed. BAL microscopy with Calcofluor white revealed septate mycelium, similar to Aspergillus. IPA was diagnosed. Therapy with voriconazole 400 mg/per day was started. A short-term clinical effect was obtained. A week after the start of antimycotic therapy, clinical and CT-deterioration, an increase in respiratory failure was noted. The dose of voriconazole was increased to 600 mg/per day. Bronchoscopy was performed. "Monfluo" test in BAL was positive. Co-trimoxazole 12 mg/kg/per day was added to therapy. Positive clinical dynamics was achieved after three days of combined therapy. In November 2016 there were no laboratory signs of IA progression, but changes on CT scan remained. The dose of voriconazole is reduced to 400 mg/day. Co-trimoxazole is continued in a prophylactic dose. Cytostatic chemotherapy (BEACOPP baz) was conducted. Remission of Hodgkin's lymphoma was achieved. Finding(s): In hematological patients with severe lymphocytopenia after cytostatic therapy, the combination of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis and pneumocystis pneumonia is possible. Early antimycotic therapy and secondary prophylaxis allows to continue the treatment of hematologic malignancies.
